From 1c34ce30590df03a7d11a49215efbe6f850e7d7d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Roman Janota <Roman.Janota@cesnet.cz>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 17:49:48 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] notifd UPDATE defer srsn start/stop to a thread

The configuration is applied in the done callback of
ietf-subscribed-notifications, which started and stopped the srsn
subscriptions in place. An on-change srsn subscription takes the change
subscription lock that the originator of a change keeps read-locked
across its done event, so (un)subscribing it from that callback stalls
until the originator's timeout. The dispatch loop unsubscribed an ended
subscription with the state lock held, which closes the same cycle.

Neither existing thread can take the work over. The thread processing
the notifd sysrepo events must not wait for the lock, another originator
may hold it read-locked while waiting for its callback on that very
thread. The dispatch loop must not either, an srsn writer holding up an
originator may be blocked on a pipe only the loop drains. A dedicated
control thread with its own session now starts and stops the srsn
subscriptions, without the state lock.

The callbacks and the dispatch loop queue the stops, a stop detaches the
pipe at once and carries the excluded count over. The configuration may
change while the thread subscribes, so a start is only marked on the
receiver and picked up by the thread with a copy of the subscription
parameters, and a start superseded by a newer one, by a stop or by
deleting the receiver is discarded. Once its srsn subscription exists, a
receiver is connected and sent subscription-started, or sent
subscription-modified if it is an active one being resubscribed, then
its pipe is dispatched.

Configuration is therefore applied asynchronously, a receiver becomes
active only after sr_apply_changes() returns.

Upstream: https://github.com/sysrepo/sysrepo/commit/3c25ebe4efc7fe9a851b7429873898cf4fb33feb
Signed-off-by: Mattias Walström <lazzer@gmail.com>
